  "query_preview": "..The center of a broad closed low is approaching the\nCalifornia coast this evening, with Utah and southwest Wyoming\nunder a southerly flow out ahead of it. This has allowed deep\ntropical moisture to move into the forecast area, with satellite\nderived PWs primarily in the 0.6 to 0.7 inch range, which is above\nthe 90th percentile for PW for this time of year. The position of\nthe upper jet on the front side of the system has brought a good\namount of instability, particularly for southwest Utah, with\nenough shear to promote some organized convection. One earlier\nstorm produced golf ball size hail, but flash flooding has been\nthe primary threat, and will continue to be through tomorrow.\n\nConvection is currently in a relative lull, though at least some\nshowers and thunderstorms will continue over southern Utah\novernight. Meanwhile, the main low will move onshore tomorrow,\nsplitting as it does so. One piece is expected to eject northward\nacross Utah and southwest Wyoming late Thursday afternoon through\nearly Friday with the other piece moving into northern Mexico. By\nthe early afternoon, convection is expected to become more intense\nagain over southern Utah, with the moisture and instability\nspreading northward into northern portions of the area with the\nmovement of the trough. Conditions will remain quite favorable for\nflash flooding over central and southern Utah, and thus the Flood\nWatch remains in effect through early Friday. \n\nAs the trough moves through, it will bring colder air along with\nit, resulting in highs 5-10F below seasonal normals Friday\nafternoon. The colder air will also bring snow levels down to\naround 9,000 feet, bringing accumulating snow to the highest\nterrain of northern Utah, though most likely not significant\namounts. Drier conditions will follow beginning Friday as the\nnorth piece of the trough ejects out of the area, but the presence\nof the southern branch of the low will keep temperatures on the\ncool side through the weekend. The southern branch of the system\nis also likely to bring a few showers to at least southeast Utah\nSaturday into Sunday as it tracks east across the Desert\nSouthwest.\n\nWeak ridging is then expected to move over Utah and southwest\nWyoming to start the upcoming work week, bringing a bit of warming\nto the area. However, with no blocking ridge in place off the\ncoast, the storm track is expected to remain active in some\nfashion next week, though there remains significant ensemble\nspread regard to the timing and details of any systems, along with\ntheir potential impacts for the forecast area.",
